+## Five-million-Product characterization
+
+These short diagnostics preceded the client-preparation fix described below;
+they are not sustainable throughput limits, soak results or acceptance passes.
+Keep workload correctness and resource
+safety separate from performance observations; do not select a passing
+latency or duration after observing the result.
+
+The measured deployment retained five million baseline Products in one Git
+repository. An additional 257 Products authored a deliberately unbalanced
+256:1:0 membership across hot, cold and empty categories in that same repository.
+Push load added one Category manifest per commit to its `main` branch.
+Both API replicas and both controllers shared three Scylla nodes and exactly
+one native Git service. The restored application images came from
+`34a7434c12778cebda2ffce906a3f473b707a9af`; these measurements do not benchmark
+subsequent application changes.
+
+The host was an Apple M3 Pro with 11 logical CPUs and 36 GiB RAM, running
+macOS 26.6.1. Docker exposed eight CPUs and 15.63 GiB RAM with the `overlayfs`
+storage driver. Each Scylla container had a 4 GiB memory limit and an 8 GiB
+memory-plus-swap limit. Clients used loopback HTTP through Docker, with local
+Git objects borrowed from a live-HEAD-verified owned seed. This excludes cold
+network-clone cost and does not measure a WAN, network bandwidth ceiling,
+GPU performance or a different storage device.
+
+Each campaign offered load for 120 seconds using the companion push driver,
+with a bounded worker pool and a 30-second drain. A busy pool dropped offers
+instead of accumulating an unbounded queue. Preparation was outside the offer
+window. No compilation or profiling ran alongside these campaigns; periodic
+Docker-stat, controller-metric and disk sampling still adds diagnostic overhead.
+
+| Measurement | One worker | Four workers |
+| --- | ---: | ---: |
+| Offered pushes/s | 2 | 8 |
+| Scheduled / offered | 240 / 240 | 960 / 960 |
+| Acknowledged | 74 | 66 |
+| Acknowledgements per declared load second | 0.617 | 0.550 |
+| Failed offers | 0 | 662 |
+| Dropped offers | 166 | 232 |
+| Successful ACK latency p50 / p95 / p99 | 1,041 / 2,692 / 16,195 ms | 1,077 / 1,913 / 20,060 ms |
+| Drain duration | 0.024 s | 1.162 s |
+| Elapsed including client preparation and observation | 159.6 s | 298.2 s |
+| Minimum sampled host free disk | 101.10 GiB | 95.76 GiB |
+
+Neither campaign missed scheduled offer times, was cancelled or exceeded its
+drain bound. Both nevertheless failed the driver's zero-failure/zero-drop
+acceptance assertions; retaining their evidence does not turn them into passes.
+The four-worker failures were local staging failures: three `add/deadline`
+and 659 `add/command_failure`. These are per-offer counts, not 662 independent
+server failures. Successful-ACK percentiles exclude failed and dropped offers.
+The observations therefore cannot establish a native Git throughput ceiling.
+
+The root cause was a configured sparse checkout retaining a 520 MB full
+on-disk index after cloning. Concurrent first staging operations exceeded
+their command bounds and left index locks; subsequent offers failed on those
+locks. Preparation now explicitly persists the sparse index within the same
+preparation deadline, without extending push-operation deadlines. A regression
+checks the on-disk index before a status command can normalize it. Bounded
+functional verification prepared four 301-byte indexes, staged concurrently
+twice and completed eight authenticated pushes, including rebases, with no
+failures, retained index locks or baseline Product-tree changes. This verifies
+the observed failure's correction, not sustained-load capacity.
+
+Post-restore reconciliation remained active, with roughly 4.7-4.9 million queued
+Products per replica at campaign boundaries. One replica's queue grew during
+the four-worker campaign despite completed reconciliations, so aggregate
+success counters do not prove backlog convergence. This is not a warm
+steady-state comparison. Runtime identities stayed fixed and no container
+restart or OOM kill occurred during either campaign.
+
+At the end of these campaigns, both APIs returned matching snapshots for all 257 fixture
+Products, with current Ready conditions and the expected resolved category
+references and paths. However, all three category-product connections on
+both replicas returned `PROJECTION_NOT_READY`; this was unavailable membership,
+not an empty result. An initially bounded audit was incomplete. A subsequent
+completed, category-scoped audit scanned 5,000,258 authoritative Products and
+verified matching expected/actual fingerprints for all 257 forward and reverse
+membership rows. Confirmed operator completion then published readiness.
+Both APIs now fully page the exact 256:1:0 memberships in both directions,
+with matching materialized counts and current Product readiness. The operator
+completion did not rewrite authoritative resources or restart core services.
+Requeuing one quarantined, owned category conflict also restored both
+controllers' healthy status. These functional recovery checks do not measure
+membership-read throughput or establish a warm steady state; the multi-million
+Product reconciliation backlog remains visible.
+
+For future characterization, retain this five-million, single-repository,
+unbalanced-membership workload and keep strictly more than 10 GiB host disk
+free. Record actual hardware, container limits, image/source/configuration
+identities and cold/warm state. Retain the full offered/ACK/failure/drop
+accounting, successful-ACK latency, preparation/drain time, client and service
+resource samples, queue movement and any recovery observations. The same
+resource envelope and client preparation are required for comparisons.
+Verify all fixture Products' current Ready generation and resolved references,
+then fully page category membership through both APIs before interpreting
+membership-read measurements. An authored skew or a consistent empty
+projection is insufficient. Report unavailable membership, client staging
+failures and unresolved recovery explicitly rather than inferring a passing
+capacity or soak target. Existing acceptance profiles remain separate.
+
+### Six-hour mixed-load comparison baseline
+
+The November 4-5, 2026 six-hour measurement is the recorded comparison
+baseline for future runs of this preserved catalog. **Benchmark recording and
+acceptance are separate outcomes:** the complete, correctness-verified
+measurement remains useful even though four dropped offers failed the
+zero-drop acceptance gate. Retain those drops in every comparison.
+
+The host was an Apple M3 Pro with 11 CPU cores and 36 GB RAM, rebooted before
+startup. The shared Docker memory envelope reported approximately 15.63 GiB;
+each of the three Scylla containers reported a 4 GiB limit. Topology was two
+APIs, two conflict-corrected controllers, one Git service and three Scylla
+nodes. The workload retained five million baseline Products, 257 owned
+fixtures and hot/cold/empty Category memberships of 256:1:0. It offered one
+complete membership walk per second across both APIs and one Category Git
+push every four seconds, with membership churn and correctness readbacks.
+One sparse publication clone served four bounded in-flight offers; this is
+not an independent multi-client push benchmark.
+
+| Measurement | Recorded baseline |
+| --- | --- |
+| Native push offered window | 21,600.000510 seconds |
+| Scheduled / acknowledged pushes | 5,400 / 5,396 |
+| Dropped offers / failed submitted pushes / missed schedules | 4 / 0 / 0 |
+| Offers requiring retry | 3 |
+| Acknowledgements per offered second | 0.249815 |
+| Successful push latency p50 / p95 / p99 / max | 1,830 / 4,849 / 7,813 / 26,319 ms |
+| Completed membership walks | 21,651; no failures, drops or missed schedules |
+| Membership-walk latency p50 / p95 / p99 | 10.567 / 96.428 / 176.075 ms |
+| Read offered window / phase elapsed | 21,651 / 21,662.158 seconds |
+| Both-API correctness and final fixture restoration | Verified |
+| Original Product-tree fence | Verified |
+
+The read window includes bounded push preparation and covers the complete
+native offered window. Successful-push latency excludes dropped offers and
+includes publication-gate waiting. The four drops occurred when the bounded
+client slots filled during slow publication/recovered non-fast-forward
+episodes; they are not four rejected or lost submitted Git pushes.
+Acknowledgements per offered second are measured throughput at this fixed
+setting, not a maximum sustainable capacity claim.
+
+Resource summaries below use 1,185 Docker samples over the mixed phase.
+Memory is Docker-reported usage, **not process RSS or configured allocation**.
+CPU is Docker's percentage, where 100% corresponds to one CPU core.
+Percentiles use nearest-rank sampled values, not request distributions.
+
+| Component | Memory p50 / p95 / max (MiB) | CPU p50 / p95 (%) |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| API A | 126.3 / 130.1 / 135.1 | 18.05 / 27.56 |
+| API B | 63.4 / 65.2 / 71.8 | 1.63 / 7.31 |
+| Controller A | 186.9 / 207.1 / 217.3 | 33.27 / 109.73 |
+| Controller B | 162.2 / 183.6 / 195.9 | 33.47 / 104.67 |
+| Git service | 215.7 / 238.9 / 284.5 | 82.70 / 151.45 |
+| Scylla node 1 | 2,119.7 / 2,284.5 / 2,312.2 | 34.32 / 72.92 |
+| Scylla node 2 | 2,126.8 / 2,248.7 / 2,281.5 | 27.95 / 68.07 |
+| Scylla node 3 | 2,111.5 / 2,232.3 / 2,252.8 | 27.89 / 69.41 |
+
+The minimum sampled host free disk was approximately 43.24 GiB; maximum
+allocated swap was 4 GiB and maximum sampled swap use was approximately
+3.64 GiB. Product queues remained around 4.6-5.0 million per controller.
+Their first/last exported depths were 4,825,885 / 4,766,426 on A and
+4,822,506 / 4,773,432 on B. This is a **post-reboot mixed-load baseline with
+ongoing reconciliation**, not a drained, warm steady-state baseline.
+No quantitative thermal baseline was captured, and no HA/fault or credential
+rotation acceptance is inferred.
+
+Preserve the private machine-readable baseline with the original evidence.
+It records image/configuration, source and driver hashes, workload and
+hardware identities, independent benchmark/acceptance outcomes, sampled
+Docker usage and process RSS separately, queue/CDC observations and evidence
+checksums. Retain raw resource samples, request outcomes and Prometheus
+exports; the summary is not a substitute for their coverage and attribution.
+For future comparisons, reuse the Product/Category dataset through GA and
+match hardware/VM limits, topology, client preparation/concurrency, configured
+auth providers, diagnostic overhead and recovery state. Report intentional
+changes explicitly rather than treating unlike runs as a regression.
+
 ## Local telemetry and live profiling
 
+For an explicitly owned, already populated catalog, the category diagnostic
+can consume a private JSON plan without seeding a second repository:
+
+```bash
+make capacity TARGET=category PROFILE=hierarchy MODE=diagnostic \
+  CAPACITY_CATEGORY_EXISTING_FIXTURE=/absolute/private/catalog-plan.json
+```
+
+The plan supplies loopback API/Git/Prometheus endpoints, the existing token
+file and seed/original clones, repository identity, fixture prefix, eight
+pinned core-container identities/configuration hashes, runner-source hashes,
+and a precompiled push-driver path/hash. It also supplies an absolute output
+directory, `launchByUnix` and `hardStopUnix` deadlines, four bounded read/push
+rates, and fixed phase budgets: 30-minute baseline, four 15-minute read steps,
+four 15-minute mixed steps, 30-minute confirmation and a six-hour soak.
+This path is diagnostic-only; it does not change existing acceptance gates.
+For finite preparation, set the plan's boolean `preparationOnly` to `true`.
+This runs the same preflight, a 15-minute mixed trial at the first configured
+read/push rates, and the full 30-minute confirmation at those rates, then
+restores the fixture and stops the owned stack. Both phases must be clean.
+The result explicitly records preparation scope; it does not run the ramps
+or six-hour soak and does not establish maximum sustainable capacity.
+For a setting already qualified by a successful preparation, set `soakOnly`
+to `true` instead. It runs only the full six-hour mixed phase at the first
+configured read/push rates, without repeating the baseline, ramps or
+confirmation. The flags are mutually exclusive. Preserve the preparation
+receipt and verify the same workload, binary and deployment pins before
+launch; never infer qualification from readiness alone. Both selections
+retain preflight, telemetry, both-API correctness, restoration and shutdown.
+Soak acceptance requires the full native offered window, every scheduled push
+acknowledged, complete read coverage and no failed, dropped or missed work.
+An entire measured window with verified correctness can still be retained as
+a comparison baseline when that acceptance gate fails; record both outcomes
+and preserve all failures and drops.
+Setup, drain and validation add wall time beyond the six offered-load hours.
+Omitting both flags retains the full benchmark sequence.
+Optional `configurationFilesSHA256` pins mounted configuration and identity
+file contents during runtime sampling. An `originalIndexSHA256` pin also
+protects the original clone's HEAD/index at the initial and final tree fences.
+Only the fixture's owned Product is reassigned, restored between phases, and
+checked through both APIs. The original Product tree is compared with the
+allowlisted fixture changes. Git preparation and drain are reported separately
+from its offered window; read traffic covers the full Git offered window.
